\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar Hit By Credit Woes; Bonds Get Safe Haven Bid   \b0\par\par\uc2 470 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 2007\par 10:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                          Latest   Change \par    USD/SGD               1.5220   +0.0087 \par    Overnight rate        2.50     +25 bps \par    Two-year bond yield:  2.30     -6 bps \par    10-year bond yield:   2.87     -2 bps \par    Two-year swap offer:  2.90     +1 bp \par    10-year swap offer:   3.35     unchanged \par    2-10-yr swap curve:   45 bps   -1 bp \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar plummeted Friday as escalating credit concerns prompted speculators to liquidate positions in emerging market assets.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Traders said that liquidity in global markets will remain the focal point in the near term, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will suffer if the credit woes evolve into a crisis.   \par \par \uc2 "We're watching credit markets very closely," a trader said. "The Singapore dollar may hold steady until there's news on the subprime front, but if this thing comes further undone there will be more damage."   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore manages the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within a secret trade-weighted band, and currently has a policy of modest and gradual appreciation.   \par \par \uc2 Analytical models indicate that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is near the center of the band, suggesting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar makes a sharp dive.   \par \par \uc2 United Overseas Bank\uc2  estimated that if the Singapore dollar held steady against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the greenback would have to climb to S1.5446 before hitting the edge of the policy band - the point where the MAS is likely to act.   \par \par \uc2 Singapore government bonds took a cue from U.S. Treasurys, which surged Thursday after French bank  \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  said it was unable to value three of its funds invested in collateralized debt obligations.   \par \par \uc2 BNP's struggle led to a surge in demand for cash across Europe, prompting the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to inject almost EUR100 billion into the money market.   \par \par \uc2 The MAS was the first \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia to follow the ECB's lead, with a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official stating early Friday that the MAS was ready to offer liquidity if needed.   \par \par \uc2 "We will stand ready to inject liquidity," Ong Chong Tee, deputy managing director at the MAS told reporters at a press conference. "If there are liquidity bottlenecks certainly we could be willing."   \par \par \uc2 Later Friday, the MAS injected S$1.5 billion of cash into the money market, two traders told Dow Jones Newswires.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly acts in the money market to ensure sufficient liquidity, but analysts said the cash infusion marked a symbolic gesture to reinforce Ong's statement.   \par \par \uc2 Despite the injection, the overnight deposit rate paid by banks rose 50 basis points to a high of 2.75% Friday morning as cash demand surged.   \par \par \uc2 -By John Jannarone, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4153; john.jannarone@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Roger Ng [ 10-08-07 0857GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 1163 1859   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070810e38a000y2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
