\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar Gains On GDP Surprise; Inflows Lift Bonds   \b0\par\par\uc2 472 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juli 2007\par 10:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par                          Latest   Change \par    USD/SGD               1.5170   -0.0020 \par    Overnight rate        2.25     +6 bps \par    Two-year bond yield:  2.39     -4 bps \par    10-year bond yield:   2.86     -2 bps \par    Two-year swap offer:  2.97     -1 bp \par    10-year swap offer:   3.46     -1 bp \par    2-10-yr swap curve:   49 bps   unch \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar rose to a seven-week high against the greenback Tuesday after data showed second-quarter economic output rose much faster than expected, underlining the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's fundamental value.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader said the U.S. dollar "is still heavy" against the Singapore dollar, and may continue to grind lower in coming sessions.   \par \par \uc2 Singapore's economy expanded 12.8% from the first quarter on a seasonally adjusted and annualized basis after growing a revised 8.5% in the previous three months, the Ministry of Trade & Industry said. Economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires had on average forecast a 7.8% expansion.   \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar jumped in response to the data release at 0000 GMT, with the greenback slipping to S$1.5170 from S$1.5200. It was the lowest level the U.S. dollar had touched since May 16.   \par \par \uc2 Another trader said that the U.S. dollar has ample room to fall against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the medium term without risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "This data will make it easier to justify a long Singapore dollar position," he said. "We could see more interest and I don't think there's any way the authorities will come in."   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore uses exchange rate targeting as its chief policy tool, and manages the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within a secret trading band.   \par \par \uc2 Analytical models indicate the trade-weighted Singapore dollar is near the middle of the undisclosed range, suggesting it could appreciate at least 1% before breaching the band's top.   \par \par \uc2 In the government bond market, prices rose as the strong economic data encouraged investors to seek Singapore dollar-denominated assets.   \par \par \uc2 "In the morning, we saw strength in the Singapore dollar show up in bonds and the market was well-bid into the afternoon," a fixed-income trader said.   \par \par \uc2 The trader added that interest rate swap levels stayed relatively supported as investors taking new long positions in bonds wanted to hedge against fixed-rate coupons.   \par \par \uc2 "Investors have been in the market bidding up (interest rate swaps), so there was some bond-swap widening," the trader said.   \par \par \uc2 Market-watchers will tune into a speech by Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke later Tuesday that may offer clues on the Fed's outlook on inflation.   \par \par \uc2 -By John Jannarone; Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4152; john.jannarone@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Roger Ng [ 10-07-07 0846GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 1163 1859   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070710e37a000po\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
