\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS- \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  costs impel Asia to loosen  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  grip   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kevin Yao   \par \uc2 898 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juli 2007\par 10:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2 , July 4 (Reuters) - Rising asset prices and the mounting cost of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are prompting Asian policy makers to step back from markets, setting the stage for a faster rise in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  In a region that routinely curbs \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength to help underpin exports, official concerns about higher exchange rates have been further soothed by the resilience of overseas sales this year despite a slowdown in the U.S. economy.   \par \par \uc2  Most Asian countries tightly manage their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  while trying to run an independent monetary policy.   \par \par \uc2  This can create a conflict. Tighter monetary conditions can attract capital, putting pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise, which becomes costly to control. This is known as the impossible trinity.   \par \par \uc2  "They have seen the limits of the system, though they are not going to end \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  completely by any means," said Marshall Gittler, chief Asia strategist at  \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  Private Wealth Management.   \par \par \uc2  Underlining the tolerance for higher exchange rates, Malaysia's Trade Minister Rafidah Aziz said last week that a firm ringgit had not hurt the country's exports, and local firms are taking the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength in their stride.   \par \par \uc2  Indonesian officials have said they were comfortable with a stronger rupiah.   \par \par \uc2   India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  admitted in its April policy review that "dealing with the impossible trinity of fixed exchange rates, open capital accounts and discretion in monetary policy had become more complex than before".   \par \par \uc2  For that reason, it allowed the rupee <INR=IN> to jump 10 percent versus the dollar in about three months from early March to achieve monetary policy control to fight against inflation.   \par \par \uc2  The rupee is the best-performer in Asia so far this year, although with inflation easing, India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may well tighten its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reins once again, analysts say.   \par \par \uc2  MORE DIFFICULT   \par \par \uc2  Tim Condon, head of Asia research at ING, said in theory it's impossible to manage the so-called impossible trinity, although Asian countries have tried.   \par \par \uc2  "But it's becoming more and more difficult in a globalised world," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  says India, Malaysia, Singapore, the Philippines and Thailand are all struggling to check their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  while controlling monetary policy with relatively open capital accounts.   \par \par \uc2  In theory, fixing an exchange rate alongside an independent monetary policy can only work with capital controls.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in Asia ex-Japan have doubled from 2003 to $2.4 trillion as central banks limited \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains, but the costs of piling up such reserves are mounting.   \par \par \uc2  These include the cost of mopping up the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is injected into the economy when a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the fall in the value of the dollar, which makes up the majority of most reserves.   \par \par \uc2  "The costs of reserve accumulation for a number of countries have reached levels which would appear difficult to sustain,"  \uc2 HSBC\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Richard Yetsenga said.   \par \par \uc2  He estimated the cost of accumulating \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves had reached around 4 percent gross domestic product in India, Malaysia, Philippines, Thailand and Singapore.   \par \par \uc2  ASSET-PRICE BUBBLE   \par \par \uc2  Capital is flowing into the region's high-flying stock markets while property prices are also booming.   \par \par \uc2  This raises concerns about future inflation, prompting Asia's more tolerant policy makers to tighten monetary conditions by letting \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rally.   \par \par \uc2  Analysts believe the Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> and Malaysian ringgit <MYR=> would benefit from the new policy tolerance.   \par \par \uc2  Standard Chartered has raised its end-2007 forecast on the rupiah to 8,900 per dollar from 9,000, and its June 2008 forecast to 8,700 from 8,800. It was trading around 9,000 per dollar on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2  "The economy looks relatively sound and also there are signs of tolerance for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation. We are definitely bullish on the rupiah," said Thomas Harr, the bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist.   \par \par \uc2  Asian emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have regained most of the ground lost in the 1997/98 financial crisis but analysts see room for further gains.   \par \par \uc2  "I think Asia will survive very well with exchange rates 20 or 30 percent above where they are now, so long that everybody in Asia moves at the same time," said Huw McKay, senior economist at Westpac.   \par \par \uc2  China, with reserves of more than $1.2 trillion, has also shown signs of tolerance for a faster rise in its yuan as it tried to tackle its soaring trade surplus -- a major point of contention with the United States.   \par \par \uc2  Most analysts expect the yuan to rise 5-6 percent versus the dollar this year, compared with last year's 3.4 percent.   \par \par \uc2  The yuan's rise has quickened since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  widened its daily trading band to 0.5 percent from 0.3 percent in May.   \par \par \uc2  "While I would not expect them to make any announcement of formal change in policy, it's quite reasonable that they will let the yuan to appreciate more within the band," Gittler said.   \par \par \uc2  A noticeable exception to the more tolerant \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  attitude is South Korea, where officials have voiced repeated concerns about the won's <KRW=> strength against the dollar and particularly the yen <JPYKRW=R>, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of key export rival Japan.   \par \par \uc2  In recent months, the won has hit its highest levels against both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the Asian financial crisis.       \par \par \uc2 ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SNI|SI|IF|PHD|Z|J   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070704e374000h5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
