\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-More than meets the eye in Singapore rates policy   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan   \par \uc2 831 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 2007\par 10:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, April 17 (Reuters) - Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced last week it was sticking to a three-year policy of modest monetary tightening -- just as it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar and drive money market rates to their lowest in more than a year.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's (MAS) words and actions further confused a market that has always struggled to fathom Singapore's complicated, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -based monetary settings.   \par \par \uc2 Like every other \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia, the MAS seemed to want policy to be tight and loose at the same time, to check rising asset prices on the one hand but cushion the economy from flagging electronics exports on the other.   \par \par \uc2 It suggested that, while nominally sticking to its moderately tight policy, \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and encourage the drop in short-term money market rates that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would engender.   \par \par \uc2 "Buy property. I am sure mortgage rates will soon fall," said one Singapore-based economist.   \par \par \uc2 Three-month interbank rates fell to 2.78 percent on Tuesday, their lowest level since November 2005.   \par \par \uc2 Monetary policy is conducted by guiding the Singapore dollar <SGD=> within a secret band determined by its trade-weighted levels. Since April 2004, the MAS has had a policy of allowing a gradual appreciation of the mid-point of that band.   \par \par \uc2 Notwithstanding the confirmation of that policy last week, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts said they would position for some weakness in the Singapore dollar, particularly against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of countries Singapore trades with.   \par \par \uc2 "It is surprising how dovish they were in the statement, which is backed up by the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  they have done and the short-term interest rates remaining very low," said HSBC economist Robert Prior-Wandesforde.   \par \par \uc2 Policy was designed to ensure the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained competitive to shore up exports, he said, although HSBC expects the electronics sector to recover shortly anyway.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts estimate the Singapore dollar has stuck near the top of its secret band for the past six months, underpinned by a robust economy and capital inflows into soaring property and stock markets.   \par \par \uc2 It hit a 9-1/2-year peak of 1.5110 against the U.S. dollar on April 10 -- the day of MAS's twice-yearly policy review -- but has since retreated a little. It was around 1.5130 on Tuesday. "The risk is that the Singapore dollar will underperform regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as growth risks may mean a more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  and dovish MAS," JPMorgan analyst Claudio Piron said in a note to clients.   \par \par \uc2 IMPOSSIBLE TRINITY   \par \par \uc2 The MAS's policy statement showed it was in a tight corner. It highlighted the 7.2 percent annualised first-quarter economic growth and buoyancy in the construction and service sectors.   \par \par \uc2 A potential slump in U.S. housing -- and therefore the U.S. economy -- and weak demand in the global electronics industry were risks, it said.   \par \par \uc2 For the moment, the U.S. problems remain little more than threats, so it would have been premature to ease policy. Singapore's economy is still expected to grow at its normal potential rate of 4.5 to 6.5 percent this year, while prices are likely to rise after the sales tax climbs 2 percentage points in July to 7 percent.   \par \par \uc2 However, electronics exports, the mainstay of growth, are weak, and the MAS has been struggling to keep the Singapore dollar in check. Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have swollen to $137.5 billion, roughly equalling GDP, and the MAS has gathered another $66 billion through swaps.   \par \par \uc2 Nowadays, analysts say, the MAS has stopped sterilising its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That means it buys up foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the market in exchange for Singapore dollars but lets the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remain with banks rather than issue bonds to absorb it.   \par \par \uc2 Piron reckons the ensuing decline in yields is deliberate, aimed at alleviating the upward pressure on the Singapore dollar without explicitly altering the monetary stance.   \par \par \uc2 "The other dilemma is that allowing lower interest rates could fuel an asset boom and risks of instability," he said.   \par \par \uc2 That may be the lesser evil for a country that aspires to be Asia's financial conduit and is courting multinationals that might otherwise head for Hong Kong or Shanghai.   \par \par \uc2 Singapore also has a heavy dependence on trade. Annual exports are estimated to be more than twice its economic output.   \par \par \uc2 A popular hypothesis in international economics states that if a country allows free capital movement, it can only control either its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or interest rates, not both.   \par \par \uc2 Singapore needs free movement of capital but at the same time it must ensure its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not become too uncompetitive.   \par \par \uc2 That means interest rate policy has to give. Under present circumstances, that means rates are falling, and at least some economists reckon the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- worried about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and economic risks -- is glad they are.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE-ECONOMY/RATES (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SI|ABX   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070420e34h000nq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
