\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  Dollar Gains On Perceived Lower  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Chance   \b0\par\par\uc2 478 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 2007\par 10:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                          Latest   Change \par  USD/SGD                 1.5132   -0.0035 \par  Overnight Deposit Rate  2.19%    unch \par  Two-Year Bond Yield:    2.37%    -6 bps \par  10-Year  Bond Yield:    2.79%    +2 bps \par  Two-Year Swap Offer:    2.98%    -1 bp \par  10-Year  Swap Offer:    3.29%    +2 bps \par  2-10-Year Swap Curve:   31 bps   +3 bp \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar rose against the greenback in Asia Monday as the local unit eased versus other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , reducing pressure on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , traders say.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 "The trade-weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar has come down from last week," a trader said.   \par \par \uc2 "There's room for the U.S. dollar to test the S$1.5100 level before (the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) comes in."   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore uses exchange-rate targeting as its chief policy tool, and since April 2004 has advocated a modest and gradual appreciation of the local unit according to a trade-weighted calculation.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of trading partners other than the U.S. gained against the local unit since Friday, lowering the Singapore dollar's weighted value.   \par \par \uc2 The euro reached highs near S$2.0600 early Monday from lows around S$2.0450 late Friday.   \par \par \uc2 The trader said that further strength in the Chinese yuan - linked to political pressure in the U.S. - may boost the Singapore dollar in coming sessions.   \par \par \uc2 "With the U.S. getting impatient on China and urging it to move faster on the yuan, we expect that this could yield more speculation of another revaluation this week," he said.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  apparently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week, and another trader said it sold about S$6 billion to restrain the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength.   \par \par \uc2 In the local debt market, short-dated government bonds gained following a T-bill auction that was priced at the lowest yield since September 2005.   \par \par \uc2 A S$1.9 billion auction of three-month T-bills sold at a cutoff yield of 2.20%, far below yields above 3.00% as recently as February.   \par \par \uc2 One trader said that the results were "on the low side" of expectations, but that short-term rates may remain suppressed for some time.   \par \par \uc2 "There's been aggressive bidding from the local banks that can't afford to miss a buying opportunity because T-bills are rarely traded in the secondary market," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Surging capital inflows have led new deposits to grow much faster than new loans in recent months, and banks have been compelled to match assets to the swelling liabilities.   \par \par \uc2 Singapore's M2 money supply, a broad measure that spans from cash to certificates of deposit, surged 22% in the year to February, according to the latest government data.   \par \par \uc2 -By John Jannarone, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4152; john.jannarone@dowjones.com [ 16-04-07 0812GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 1163 1859   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070416e34g000ej\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pic