\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Down As Ctrl Bk Strikes Again; Bonds Fall   \b0\par\par\uc2 441 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 April 2007\par 11:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                          Latest   Change \par  USD/SGD                 1.5197   +0.0020 \par  Overnight Deposit Rate  2.25%    unch \par  Two-Year Bond Yield:    2.37%    +1 bp \par  10-Year  Bond Yield:    2.74%    +1 bp \par  Two-Year Swap Offer:    2.97%    +4 bps \par  10-Year  Swap Offer:    3.24%    +2 bps \par  2-10-Year Swap Curve:   27 bps   -2 bps \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was down in Asia Thursday as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  returned to the market for the third time in a week to stem the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's rise.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to keep the market guessing," a trader said. But he added that the U.S. unit's new-found upward movement may have some momentum behind it, and its near-term floor "may keep getting higher and higher."   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  late Thursday morning to push the U.S. dollar up from S$1.5170 to S$1.5220, several traders said.   \par \par \uc2 Tuesday, the MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in two waves, first lifting the greenback from S$1.5115 to S$1.5185, and later from S$1.5160 to S$1.5215.   \par \par \uc2 But a greenback upside bias may now be taking hold, as minutes from the recent meeting of the U.S. Federal Reserve released overnight suggest monetary tightening remains possible.   \par \par \uc2 "If the Federal Reserve is skewed towards a hike, people may start buying into the U.S. dollar, and the base will move higher and higher, probably testing S$1.5250," another trader said.   \par \par \uc2 In its policy statement Tuesday, the MAS said the trade-weighted Singapore dollar has recently traded near the upper end of its undisclosed policy band.   \par \par \uc2 The MAS uses exchange-rate targeting as its chief policy tool, and since April 2004 has advocated a modest and gradual appreciation of the local unit.   \par \par \uc2 In the local debt market, government bonds fell, mirroring a move in U.S. Treasurys.   \par \par \uc2 "Yields were pretty low at these levels, and the move in U.S. Treasurys gave some people an excuse to sell bonds," a trader said.   \par \par \uc2 U.S Treasury yields rose one to two basis points across maturities in overnight trade.   \par \par \uc2 But another trader said Singapore's overnight deposit rate remains low enough to anchor short-dated yields in coming sessions.   \par \par \uc2 "Even if yields creep up in the U.S., the market here is still flushed with cash so bonds aren't likely to sell off," the trader said.   \par \par \uc2 -By John Jannarone, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4152; john.jannarone@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Paul Baylis [ 12-04-07 0929GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 1163 1859   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070412e34c000g8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
