\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Won, peso firm, Sing dlr down on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  fears   \b0\par\par\uc2 776 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2007\par 04:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2 , April 11 (Reuters) - The Philippine peso touched a six-year high due to strong remittance inflows on Wednesday, while the South Korean won pushed higher as heavy foreign buying helped lift the local stock market index to a record high.   \par \par \uc2 In contrast, the Singapore dollar retreated from Tuesday's 9-1/2-year peak amid jitters about \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb its strength.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a theme across Asia. The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of buying U.S. dollars to cap the peso's gains and dealers in India, Malaysia and South Korea were all watchful of the authorities.   \par \par \uc2 The Thai baht <THB=TH> was steady at about 34.95 per dollar onshore after the Bank of Thailand delivered a 50-basis-point cut in the one-day repurchase rate to 4.00 percent, in line with expectations.   \par \par \uc2 The cut, aimed at boosting the economy, is also seen as a measure to weaken the baht, which has remained strong despite capital controls aimed at pushing it down. The baht has risen just over 3 percent this year after a 14 percent increase versus the dollar in 2006.   \par \par \uc2 "They should have done this before the capital controls were introduced and it would have been a better way to curb speculative flows and slow \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength," said UBS \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Nizam Idris.   \par \par \uc2 The peso hit 47.90 per dollar <PHP=>, a six-year high, as remittances that had bunched up over the Easter holiday continued to push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher.   \par \par \uc2 But suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stalled the gains, dealers said.   \par \par \uc2 "Basically it's still strong remittances," said one trader in Manila, explaining the peso's rise. "We believe they (the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) were there -- that's why the market has had a hard time piercing 47.90."   \par \par \uc2 SING DOLLAR DOWN   \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=> fell as much as 0.36 percent to 1.5203 per U.S. dollar, nearing a three-week low hit on Tuesday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of heavy U.S. dollar-buying.   \par \par \uc2 It had hit a 9-1/2-year peak earlier on Tuesday at 1.5110.   \par \par \uc2 Some dealers said it was possible that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was in the market again early on Wednesday after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Tuesday's London and Asian sessions.   \par \par \uc2 "They bought about 2.5 to 2.7 billion U.S. dollars in the global day yesterday and I think they are in again this morning," said one trader.   \par \par \uc2 But another trader said it was likely that the market was covering short positions on the U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Stronger-than-expected economic growth and a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision to stick to a policy of modest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation had boosted the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar on Tuesday, before the apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 With regional equity markets bouncing back from last month's global volatility, analysts said rising risk appetite was likely to boost \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  across Asia.   \par \par \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Ben Simpfendorfer said net buying across Asian equity markets in the past week was its strongest in 12 months -- a sign that carry trades were back in play.   \par \par \uc2 Foreign investors were net buyers of South Korean shares for a seventh consecutive session on Wednesday, bringing their total buying over this period to $1.52 billion. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0813 GMT    \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       119.17         119.14       -0.03\par  Sing dlr        1.5170         1.5148       -0.15\par  Taiwan dlr      33.094         33.088       -0.02\par  Korean won      932.30         934.00       +0.18\par  Baht             34.96          34.93       -0.09\par  Peso             47.96          47.95       -0.01\par  Rupiah         9093.00        9096.00       +0.03\par  Rupee            42.81          42.91       +0.23\par  Ringgit         3.4490         3.4460       -0.09\par  Yuan            7.7270         7.7340       +0.09\par  Change so far in 2007\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       119.17         119.01       -0.13\par  Sing dlr        1.5170         1.5337       +1.10\par  Taiwan dlr      33.094         32.585       -1.54\par  Korean won      932.30        0929.50       -0.30\par  Baht             34.96          36.08       +3.20\par  Peso             47.96          49.03       +2.24\par  Rupiah         9093.00        8988.00       -1.15\par  Rupee            42.81          44.26       +3.38\par  Ringgit         3.4490         3.5270       +2.26\par  Yuan            7.7270         7.8051       +1.01\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-