\par\par \b \uc2 = 2nd UPDATE: Singapore Keeps SGD Policy; 1Q GDP Tops Views   \b0\par\par\uc2 965 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 2007\par 05:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2007, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 Apr 09, 2007 (DJCS via Comtex) --   \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments from economists, breakdown of first quarter GDP, background.)   \par \par \uc2 By John Jannarone   \par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintained its slightly restrictive monetary policy, and economists say the stance may remain through the year despite an outlook for slower economic growth.   \par \par \uc2 At its first semiannual review of 2007, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore reaffirmed its preference for a modest and gradual appreciation of the Singapore dollar - a policy in place since April 2004.   \par \par \uc2 First quarter economic growth figures, also released Tuesday, confirmed economists' views that the economy decelerated, but the growth was faster-than-expected.   \par \par \uc2 On a seasonally adjusted and annualized basis, the economy expanded 7.2% from the fourth quarter, after growing 7.9% in the previous three months. A Dow Jones Newswires poll had forecast a 3.7% expansion.   \par \par \uc2 Citigroup economist Chua Hak Bin said the modest deceleration in growth from the fourth quarter is an encouraging sign as it shows the economy "has held up well despite a slowdown in the electronics cycle."   \par \par \uc2 He expects the economy to grow 6.2% in 2007, slower than 7.9% in 2006, but near the top end of the government's 4.5% to 6.5% target.   \par \par \uc2 Compared with a year earlier, the economy grew 6.0%, slower than the 6.6% growth in the fourth quarter. The Dow Jones poll forecast on-year growth of 5.4%.   \par \par \uc2 Singapore's exports and manufacturing were weak in the first quarter due to softer demand from the U.S. for the country's mainstay electronic products.   \par \par \uc2 Manufacturing output grew 6.1% in the first quarter from a year earlier, slowing from 7.7% in the fourth quarter, with strength in ship and oil rig construction helping offset weak electronics.   \par \par \uc2 But investment in transport and marine equipment, which contributed nearly three percentage points to economic growth in the fourth quarter, may not be sustainable in 2007, said HSBC economist Robert Prior-Wandesforde.   \par \par \uc2 "The growth in the first quarter is still very much driven by transport equipment," he said.   \par \par \uc2 "I do think the area is vulnerable and if transport equipment investment does fall back at some point, it would leave the economy exposed."   \par \par \uc2 The services sector grew 6.1%, down modestly from 6.6% in the fourth quarter, while construction output surged 7.0% after posting only 2.7% growth in the fourth quarter.   \par \par \uc2 Improved construction output partly reflected the recovery in Singapore's residential and commercial property markets.   \par \par \uc2 Current Policy Offers Cushion   \par \par \uc2 Most analysts predicted the MAS would leave its policy unchanged, and many contend the stance will hold again at the next review in October.   \par \par \uc2 They say the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which the MAS manages within a secret band, has sufficient room to depreciate without an outright change in overall policy.   \par \par \uc2 "The MAS will have flexibility to allow a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  without a change," Prior-Wandesforde said.   \par \par \uc2 In its statement, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed a widely-held view that the trade weighted Singapore dollar has fluctuated near the top of its policy band since October.   \par \par \uc2 Prior-Wandesforde said that economy is "not quite weak enough" to warrant a shift to a more accommodative policy.   \par \par \uc2 HSBC forecasts 4.9% growth in 2007, but Prior-Wandesforde said the target "is beginning to look a bit pessimistic."   \par \par \uc2 But if demand for Singapore's key electronics exports doesn't rebound in the second half, some say there's a chance the MAS will opt for monetary stimulus.   \par \par \uc2 Standard Chartered economist Joseph Tan said that Singapore's trade-dependent economy remains closely tied to the U.S., where downside risks are substantial.   \par \par \uc2 "The U.S. economy is not looking that hot," Tan said.   \par \par \uc2 "Whether or not the second half is going to look weak depends on the U.S. economy."   \par \par \uc2 Standard Chartered expects the U.S. economy to expand by 1.7% in 2007, but says the chance of a recession could prompt the MAS to switch to a neutral stance for the Singapore dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Ultra-low Inflation   \par \par \uc2 Tan said the MAS isn't expecting major inflationary pressure to result from an increase in the goods and services tax to 7% from 5%, due to take effect in July.   \par \par \uc2 The MAS said a two percentage point increase in the GST will add 0.5 percentage points to the CPI in 2007 and 2008.   \par \par \uc2 Tan said the last hike to 5% from 3% resulted in a 0.8 percentage point increase in the inflation rate.   \par \par \uc2 "The passthrough effect is not going to be as strong as the previous round - at least the MAS is not expecting it to be," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Tan said if prices for imports like fuel decline before October, the MAS may have even greater scope for looser policy.   \par \par \uc2 Following the GDP data and policy statement, the U.S. dollar fell to around S$1.5115 from S$1.5130 just before the announcement, but the momentum quickly faded.   \par \par \uc2 One trader said the stronger-than-expected growth figures pressured the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar to appreciate, but the chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  prevented a sustained rise.   \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar has dropped to its lowest level against the Singapore dollar since 1997 in recent weeks, and analysts expect further declines in the months ahead.   \par \par \uc2 UBS expects the U.S. dollar to reach S$1.4800 by the end of the year, as encouraging growth prospects continue to draw capital inflows.   \par \par \uc2 The MAS primarily targets the exchange rate rather than domestic interest rates because import prices are the main driver of inflation in the city-state.   \par \par \uc2 -By John Jannarone, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4152; john.jannarone@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Stephen Wright   \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires   \par \par \uc2 04-09-07 2300ET   \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020070410e34a000bf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
