\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Highest Since '97 On Weak USD, Strong MYR, CNY  \b0\par\par\uc2 463 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2007\par 09:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                         Latest   Change \par  USD/SGD                1.5174   -0.0069 \par  Overnight Rate         2.69%    unch \par  Two-Year Bond Yield:   2.44%    -5 bps \par  10-Year  Bond Yield:   2.86%    -3 bps \par  Two-Year Swap Offer:   2.97%    -8 bps \par  10-Year  Swap Offer:   3.21%    -5 bps \par  2-10-Year Swap Curve:  24 bps   +3 bps \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar reached its highest level since 1997 in Asian trade Thursday on weakness in the U.S. dollar and strength in the Malaysian ringgit and the Chinese yuan.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Traders need not worry about volatility in the market for the next week as the central banks in Malaysia and \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate too fast, said Alvin Liew, an economist from United Overseas Bank.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore has a policy of modest and gradual appreciation of the Singapore dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback weakened against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Wednesday's Federal Open Market Committee statement, and the Singapore dollar "went along with the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and strengthened against the U.S. dollar," said Liew.  \par \par \uc2 After announcing its widely expected decision to keep interest rates at 5.25% for a sixth straight meeting, the Fed softened its tightening bias and referred to "future policy adjustments" rather than specifying the possibility of future rate hikes as it has in past post-decision statements.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar also tracked the strength of the yuan and the ringgit. The U.S. dollar hit a post-reevaluation low of CNY7.7297 and touched a new nine-year low of MYR3.4570.  \par \par \uc2 Liew said the U.S. dollar would likely stay in the S$1.5100-$1.5200 range for the next week, with very strong support at S$1.5122, as the MAS is expected to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this level.  \par \par \uc2 Another local bank dealer said the MAS would likely only slow the pair's fall and wouldn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  too aggressively to avoid volatility in the market.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  government bonds rallied, tracking the U.S. Treasurys, which rose after the Fed softened its tightening bias.  \par \par \uc2 "There's not much key data in the coming sessions, so I would expect to see some position readjustments in the Singapore bonds if the U.S. Treasurys move sideways in the near term," said a local bond dealer.  \par \par \uc2 He said the market would have to readjust the positions to accommodate S$2.9 billion worth of new bonds in April.  \par \par \uc2 The MAS announced the size of the next five-year Singapore bond auction after the market closed Wednesday. The amount was near the top end of market expectations.  \par \par \uc2 -By Wei Yi Lim, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4036; weiyi.lim@dowjones.com [ 22-03-07 0815GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070322e33m000f1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
