he Singapore dollar also tracked the strength of the yuan and the ringgit. The U.S. dollar hit a post-reevaluation low of CNY7.7297 and touched a new nine-year low of MYR3.4570. Liew said the U.S. dollar would likely stay in the S$1.5100-$1.5200 range for the next week, with very strong support at S$1.5122, as the MAS is expected to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this level. Another local bank dealer said the MAS would likely only slow the pair's fall and wouldn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  too aggressively to avoid volatility in the market. \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  government bonds rallied, tracking the U.S. Treasurys, which rose after the Fed softened its tightening bias. 'There's not much key data in the coming sessions, so I would expect to see some position readjustments in the Singapore bonds if the U.S. Treasurys move sideways in the near term,' said a local bond dealer. He said the market would have to readjust the positions to accommodate S$2.9 billion worth of new bonds in April. The MAS announced the size of the next five-year Singapore bond auction after the market closed Wednesday. The amount was near the top end of market expectations. -By Wei Yi Lim, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4036; weiyi.lim@dowjones.com -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020070322e33m004s9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
