\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar Steady On Two-Way Interest; Bonds Rally  \b0\par\par\uc2 373 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2007\par 08:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                         Latest   Change \par  USD/SGD                1.5263   -0.0010 \par  Overnight rate         2.47%    -0.41 \par  Two-year bond yield:   2.84%    -5 bps \par  10-year bond yield:    3.02%    -7 bps \par  Two-year swap offer:   3.35%    -5 bps \par  10-year swap offer:    3.49%    -4 bps \par  2-10-year swap curve:  14 bps   +1 bp \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar traded sideways in Asia on Monday, with capital outflows from the weak stock market offset by some investors who bought the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  because of the local economy's strong prospects.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 "Domestic equities are still selling down, so there's capital outflow," said Selena Ling, head of treasury research at Oversea-Chinese Banking Corp.  \par \par \uc2 "At the same time, there are people who are buying the Singapore dollar, so the two-way interest offsets each other."  \par \par \uc2 A local bank dealer said investors who are bullish about the Singapore economy are taking this opportunity to buy the Singapore dollar despite an equities sell-down, resulting in a tight range in Monday's trade.  \par \par \uc2 The Straits Times Index fell below 2940 for the first time this year, with all 50 component stocks in the negative territory.  \par \par \uc2 Ling said people are waiting for more U.S. data later in the global session.  \par \par \uc2 "If there's good data, the selling of Wall Street may reverse slightly, and the Asian outlook will be more positive as well," she said.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. ISM non-manufacturing index is due at 1500 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 The local bank dealer said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless the pair falls to S$1.5200.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers expect the pair to trade between S$1.5210 and S$1.5350 for the rest of the week.  \par \par \uc2 The MAS operates monetary by managing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an undisclosed trading band.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore government bond prices were higher across the board.  \par \par \uc2 "The bond market is traditionally a safe haven," said a local bond dealer.  \par \par \uc2 "The key's still the equity market. If equities continue to fall tomorrow, bond prices will continue to rise."  \par \par \uc2 -By Wei Yi Lim, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4036; weiyi.lim@dowjones.com [ 05-03-07 0703GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070305e3350008y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
