\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Singapore dollar gets GDP boost, rest of Asia firm  \b0\par\par\uc2 840 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Februar 2007\par 03:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Feb 14 (Reuters) - The Singapore dollar rose on Wednesday following the release of stronger-than-expected economic growth numbers, but gains were tempered as the market remained wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also edged up against the U.S. dollar, supported by rallying stock markets, but trade was subdued ahead of U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke's congressional testimony, which starts later in the day, and Japanese gross domestic product data on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar rose as far as 1.5365 per U.S. dollar <SGD=>, up about 0.5 percent from Tuesday's one-month low, as data showed the Singapore economy grew at a faster-than-expected annualised rate of 7.9 percent in the fourth quarter.  \par \par \uc2 Economists had forecast 7.6 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "The GDP figure is good news for the Singapore dollar," said Alvin Cheng, a trader at Fortis Bank Hong Kong Branch.  \par \par \uc2 There was some caution as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) was suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively in the market on Monday to cap local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength. Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was probably in the market again on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "Many traders love to sell dollar/Sing but the MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  two days ago was very aggressive," Cheng said.  \par \par \uc2 "The Sing dollar is definitely a buy. People are waiting for dollar/Sing to move higher before selling for fear of running into MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 The 2007/08 \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  budget on Thursday was the next focus for local markets.  \par \par \uc2 "Downside risks in the budget for the Singapore dollar include a one percent corporate tax cut instead of the expected 2 percent and increases to stamp duties or other negative changes to property-related items," United Overseas Bank said in a note.  \par \par \uc2 A FRESH STEER  \par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were expected to take their direction from the Bernanke testimony and Japan's fourth quarter GDP data.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese figures could shed light on the interest rate outlook. At the moment, analysts are split on whether the Bank of Japan will raise rates at a meeting next week.  \par \par \uc2 "Everything is focused on the Q4 GDP number. The market is trying to hang its hat on that and it is a nice target but I'm not sure if it will be conclusive," said Ben Simpfendorfer, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Royal Bank of Scotland in Hong Kong.  \par \par \uc2 "The Taiwan dollar is interesting -- it is increasingly the region's funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and so what happens tonight and tomorrow could be important for it."  \par \par \uc2 The Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP> briefly touched a 3-1/2-month low at 33.098 per U.S. dollar before turning round, helped by stock market gains and a firmer yen.  \par \par \uc2 In India the rupee <INR=IN> was pushed higher after the Reserve Bank of India raised the cash reserve ratio -- the percentage of banks' total deposits they have to keep with it -- to 6.0 percent from 5.5 percent on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The increase will take place in two stages, the first on Feb. 17 and the second on March 3, and follows a quarter-point increase in the main lending rate in January.  \par \par \uc2 "With the pace of monetary tightening having now ratcheted up, the chances of a meaningful correction in asset markets must be increasing," HSBC economist Robert Prior-Wandesforde said in a note.  \par \par \uc2 "The key issue is that demand growth has been exceeding supply for a long time now, largely as a result of a monetary policy that has been far too loose," he said in the note.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee was trading at about 44.13 per dollar, up about a fifth of a percent from Tuesday's close. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0808 GMT   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       121.17         121.44       +0.22\par  Sing dlr        1.5363         1.5421       +0.38\par  Taiwan dlr      32.981         33.081       +0.30\par  Korean won      938.30         939.40       +0.12\par  Baht             35.74          35.76       +0.06\par  Peso             48.36          48.50       +0.29\par  Rupiah         9068.00        9072.00       +0.04\par  Rupee            44.14          44.22       +0.18\par  Ringgit         3.4965         3.5000       +0.10\par  Yuan            7.7585         7.7584       -0.00\par  Change so far in 2007\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       121.17         119.01       -1.78\par  Sing dlr        1.5363         1.5337       -0.17\par  Taiwan dlr      32.981         32.585       -1.20\par  Korean won      938.30        0929.50       -0.94\par  Baht             35.74          36.08       +0.95\par  Peso             48.36          49.03       +1.39\par  Rupiah         9068.00        8988.00       -0.88\par  Rupee            44.14          44.26       +0.27\par  Ringgit         3.4965         3.5270       +0.87\par  Yuan            7.7585  