\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dollar Steady Before G7; Bonds Mixed  \b0\par\par\uc2 319 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2007\par 09:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2007, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                         Latest   Change \par  USD/SGD                1.5325   unch \par  Overnight rate         2.94%    unch \par  Two-year bond yield:   2.93%    -4 bps \par  10-year bond yield:    3.16%    -1 bp \par  Two-year swap offer:   3.48%    +2 bps \par  10-year swap offer:    3.57%    +1 bp \par  2-10-year swap curve:  9 bps    -1 bp \par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar remained steady against the greenback Friday as traders wait anxiously to see whether the weekend summit of the Group of Seven major industrial nations results in a strong call for the appreciation of Japanese yen. 'It's heavy on the USD/SGD at S$1.5300 strong support for the day,' said Emmanuel Ng, a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for the Overseas Chinese Banking Corp. 'If we do get explicit pressure for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation, then there would be a top-heavy trend. (The pair)may try again on the downside.' Ng predicts the USD/SGD to range from S$1.5250 to S$1.5400 next week. However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at S$1.5280, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar reaches the top end of its undisclosed trading band, he said. Another foreign bank dealer said he would recommend selling on rally at S$1.5380. In the Singapore bonds market, there was a little buying in shorter-dated bonds on fund flows from the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Otherwise, bond trading will remain quiet until the market reopens Feb. 21 after the Chinese New Year holiday, said one dealer. The market is still waiting for the auction of the 20-year bond, which will likely take place after the Chinese New Year. Traders remain sidelined because they don't want to take up any position before the size of the 20-year bond is announced. -By Wei Yi Lim, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4036; weiyi.lim@dowjones.com -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020070209e329003h3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
