ll remain in range-trade."  \par \par \uc2 Another local bank dealer agreed the pair would trade in a tight range for the rest of the week.  \par \par \uc2 "Traders have no new leads at all. Everyone is focusing on the euro and the yen now, so we can only watch for the G7 this weekend," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Traders expect the pair to trade between S$1.5300 to S$1.5400 for the rest of the week.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore bond market was equally quiet, mirroring U.S Treasurys.  \par \par \uc2 However, a local bond trader said more buying can be expected in the shorter-dated bonds for the rest of the month.  \par \par \uc2 "The old seven-year bond will expire on March 1," she said. "Traders are not rushing to replace it with the new 20-year bonds, as this is the first issuance, and they do not know what the size of the bonds will be, nor what to expect. So they will replace the expiring bonds with short-end bonds."  \par \par \uc2 -By Wei Yi Lim, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4036; weiyi.lim@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Paul Baylis [ 05-02-07 0745GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070205e325000cc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
