\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dollar Flat Late, Capital Inflows Offer Support  \b0\par\par\uc2 450 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Januar 2007\par 10:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2007, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                         Latest   Change \par  USD/SGD                1.5353   +0.0003 \par  Overnight rate         3.06%    +6 bps \par  Two-year bond yield:   2.98%    -2 bps \par  10-year bond yield:    3.13%    unch \par  Two-year swap offer:   3.47%    unch \par  10-year swap offer:    3.55%    unch \par  2-10-year swap curve:  8 bps    unch \par   SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was almost flat in Asian trade Thursday, but capital inflows continue to exert upward pressure on the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , traders say. \par   'The Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (ex-Japan) are still being driven higher by strong equity-related inflows, and it is difficult to identify possible negative catalysts,' a strategist at a local bank said. \par   The prospect of an \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is preventing a breakout of the Singapore dollar from its current range, analysts said. \par   The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  controls money supply by exchange-rate targeting and manages the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  within an undisclosed, trade-weighted band. \par   At current levels, the Singapore dollar is close to the upper edge of the policy band, according to analyst estimates. \par    United Overseas Bank estimated in a note that if the greenback fell to S$1.5330, the trade-weighted Singapore dollar would be 2.5% above the midpoint, or at the stronger edge of the policy band. \par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market participants said the MAS sold the city-state's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  overnight, driving the U.S. dollar up from S$1.5335 to S$1.5380. Thursday's appreciation of the yen against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar could have lessened the likelihood of further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , they said. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar weakened to Y78.37 from around Y78.85 earlier Thursday, easing the local unit away from the top of the band. A local trader said a decline in the greenback to S$1.5325 could trigger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but said 'much depends' on the yen. He added that the U.S. dollar would need a sustained break below Y120 before the Singapore dollar could continue its trend to the upside. In the government bond market, limited buying interest supported short-dated maturities, but activity was otherwise 'very quiet,' a local trader said. 'The only real theme is the expected steepening of the yield curve in advance of the issue of the new 20-year bond,' the trader said. Singapore is scheduled to issue its first 20-year government bond on Mar. 1. The next market catalyst may be U.S. existing home sales data expected later Thursday, and players will watch for a possible reaction by U.S. Treasurys. -By Singapore Bureau; Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4150; djnews.singapore.bureau@dowjones.com -Edited by Ven Ram -0-   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020070125e31p004v2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
