\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dollar Down Slightly, Remains Near Top Of Band  \b0\par\par\uc2 444 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2007\par 11:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2007, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                         Latest   Change \par  USD/SGD                1.5374   +0.0012 \par  Overnight rate         3.00%    +6 bps \par  Two-year bond yield:   3.00%    unch \par  10-year bond yield:    3.14%    +3 bps \par  Two-year swap offer:   3.46%    +1 bp \par  10-year swap offer:    3.53%    +2 bps \par  2-10-year swap curve:  +7 bps   +1 bp \par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar sounded a weak note in Asian trade Monday after a string of gains last week, with analysts saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to resume an upward momentum as the chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains high. 'On Friday it was quite clear that the Singapore dollar was at the edge of the strong side of the band,' a strategist said. 'It corrected a little bit, but we're still bordering on the edge.' The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore uses exchange rate targeting as its chief policy tool, managing the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within an undisclosed trade-weighted band. The local unit hit its recent peak Friday, with the U.S. dollar touching a low near S$1.5340. But by Monday, the trade-weighted Singapore dollar fell some 0.5%, temporarily reducing the chance that the MAS would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , another strategist said. But analysts say even a modest decline in the greenback against the Singapore dollar could prompt the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to act. 'I think if (the U.S. dollar) reached S$1.5370, we could see the MAS turn up,' the first strategist said, adding that without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  the first support is at S$1.5340. The second strategist said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wouldn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless the greenback dipped to S$1.5325. Meanwhile, \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  government bonds fell slightly, with the yield curve steepening as a bid for shorter-dated maturities appeared mid-session, traders said. 'Yields were up at first, but there was some buying later...and the short end held up the best,' a trader said. The Singapore dollar overnight deposit rate, currently at 3.125%-3.000% remains relatively low, and may be supportive of short-dated issues in coming sessions, another trader said. 'The cost of funding is fairly low, and it's helping the short-end,' the second trader said. The MAS announced Monday that it would issue S$1.7 billion in two-year government notes in a reopening of an existing issue. However, the auction size met expectations, and didn't prompt a reaction in the market, the trader said. -By John Jannarone, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4152; john.jannarone@dowjones.com -Edited by Michael Kitchen -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020070122e31m0058x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
