\par\par \b \uc2 Thai fin min: job of halting baht rise done-report  \b0\par\par\uc2 374 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 2007\par 04:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Jan 20 (Reuters) - Thailand had no choice but to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the rise of the baht, a job that has now been done, the country's finance minister said in an interview published in the Singapore Straits Times newspaper on Saturday.  \par \par \uc2 Pridiyathorn Devakula, also Thailand's deputy prime minister, reiterated the threat to exports posed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise, the original reason given for capital controls imposed on Dec. 18.  \par \par \uc2 "Exports comprise 60 percent of gross domestic product. Thailand owes its growth to exports," he told the newspaper.  \par \par \uc2 Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced rules on Dec. 18 targeting short-term speculators with limits to the amount of money they could pull out of the country within a year of investing it.  \par \par \uc2 The rules were diluted the next day after a near 15 percent one-day fall in the Thai stock market forced the bank into a U-turn.  \par \par \uc2 Despite the market reaction, and strong criticism of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  from investors, Pridiyathorn said he had to give bank governor Tarisa Watanagase full credit for taking the strong measures necessary to curb the baht's rise, the Strait Times reported.  \par \par \uc2 The Thai minister said China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls were part of the reason for having to arrest the rise of the baht.  \par \par \uc2 "If China allows its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate, Thailand would not have to do this," he told the newspaper. Pridiyathorn also defended changes to the Foreign Business Act aimed at limiting the ability of foreign companies to control Thai firms through the use of nominees.  \par \par \uc2 He said that if a police investigation found that Singapore's state investment firm Temasek Holdings had used nominees to exert control over Shin Corp., other companies in the same boat would be forced to hurriedly sell off shares.  \par \par \uc2 "I had to prevent that nightmare," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The rules ban foreign firms from owning 50 percent or more of Thai companies, but nominees are often used to gain de facto control. Thai police are investigating whether Temasek followed this route after buying its stake in Shin Corp. from ousted former prime minister Thaksin Shinawatra.  \par \par \uc2 THAILAND-FINMIN/|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|RNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070120e31k000cg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
