\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  Dollar Down,  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Suspected; Bonds Weak  \b0\par\par\uc2 532 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2007\par 08:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2007, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                         Latest   Change \par  USD/SGD                1.5463   +0.0076 \par  Overnight rate         3.00%    -25 bps \par  Two-year bond yield:   2.95%    +1 bp \par  10-year bond yield:    3.01%    +3 bps \par  Two-year swap offer:   3.43%    +2 bps \par  10-year swap offer:    3.44%    +2 bps \par  2-10-year swap curve:  +1 bp    unch \par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was weaker in Asian trade Friday because of suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and on generally bullish U.S. dollar sentiment due to a soft Japanese yen and euro. 'The Singapore NEER (net effective exchange rate) was at the top of the band, so something definitely had to be done,' said a foreign bank dealer. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore operates monetary policy by keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within an undisclosed trading band. The dealer said the performance of the pair in the short term will be dependent on the Bank of Japan's interest rate decision next week. 'If there's no rate hike, the dollar-yen will go higher, and the dollar-Sing will have to follow suit,' he said. Another local bank dealer was optimistic about the greenback in the short term. 'We will see some upward bias (for USD/SGD) next week and it may test S$1.5500,' he said. 'The (U.S.) dollar is bullish now, but I think it is only for the time being.' CMC Markets analyst Ashraf Laidi said the most immediate downside risk to the U.S. dollar remains a rebound in the oil price. Crude oil futures fell below US$52 per barrel for the first time since May 2005 on Thursday in the U.S. If the price rose to US$57-US$59, the euro would likely see a resurgence of buyers and the U.S. dollar would likely be capped at Y121.00, he said. In the Singapore bond market, prices fell, tracking the global bond market. A local bond dealer said the Bank of England's surprise interest rate hike and 'reasonably good data' in the U.S. overnight caused bond prices to fall globally. The U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics reported initial unemployment claims fell 26,000 to 299,000 in the week to Jan. 6 - the first reading below 300,000 since July 2006 and another sign of economic strength for the U.S. The dealer said bond investors will be watching the U.S. December retail sales due at 1330 GMT. A Dow Jones Newswires survey of 21 economists produced a median estimate of a 0.7% gain in sales. Next week, the Singapore market has export data to look forward to. Emmanuel Ng, a strategist from Overseas Chinese Banking Corp., said the December non-oil exports are unlikely to disappoint on the downside, considering reasonably good data from the region, such as the Taiwan exports. 'If the data doesn't disappoint on the downside, the bond yields would be fairly supported,' he said. The December non-oil exports data are due Wednesday. -By Wei Yi Lim, Dow Jones Newswires, 65-6415-4036, weiyi.lim@dowjones.com -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020070112e31c0030d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
