\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar Up Slightly, Chance Of MAS Action Higher  \b0\par\par\uc2 429 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Dezember 2006\par 09:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                        Latest   Change \par  USD/SGD               1.5368   -0.0007 \par  Overnight rate        3.37%    +24 bps \par  Two-year bond yield:  3.01%    +3 bps \par  10-year bond yield:   3.05%    -1 bp \par  Two-year swap offer:  3.40%    +2 bps \par  10-year swap offer:   3.42%    +1 bp \par  2-10-year swap curve: +2 bps   -1 bp \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar gained modestly on light volume in Asian trade Thursday but lost momentum as the possibility of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  increased, traders said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 "I'm not sure the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar will hold here," a trader said. "The (trade-weighted) exchange rate is pretty high, and I think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could come into the market."  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore uses the exchange rate as its chief policy tool, managing the local unit within an undisclosed, trade-weighted band.  \par \par \uc2 Early in the Asian day, the trade-weighted Singapore dollar gained from Wednesday, but retraced some of its rise to about 1.0% above the center of the band, DBS \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Philip Wee said.  \par \par \uc2 Wee estimates that if the U.S. dollar fell to S$1.5290 - with the Singapore dollar steady against other basket \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  - the trade-weighted unit would reach the top of the band, about 1.5% over the midpoint.  \par \par \uc2 But traders say the MAS recently acted when the unit was inside the band's upper limit and that there is significant chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at current levels.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  government bond yields opened three to four basis points higher after yields on U.S. Treasurys rose five to six basis points overnight on stronger-than-expected housing data in the world's largest economy.  \par \par \uc2 But buyers in the local market later reversed much of the losses, reflecting year-end volatility that may extend through Friday.  \par \par \uc2 "There was late bottom-picking that brought the long end back toward yesterday's levels," a trader said. "The market is thin, and it doesn't take much to move it."  \par \par \uc2 Speculators' modest appetite for government paper was forceful enough to drive yields below the day's opening levels, the trader said.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. November existing home sales data, due later Thursday, could have an impact on fixed-income markets, but only on a major surprise.  \par \par \uc2 "It would have to deviate far from what the market has already positioned for," the trader said.  \par \par \uc2 -By John Jannarone, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4152; john.jannarone@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Ven Ram [ 28-12-06 0820GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020061228e2cs0007x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
