he upper half of the MAS' target range, and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains possible, the strategist said. He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to minimize drastic fluctuations in the U.S. dollar-\b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar exchange rate, even if its market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  have only short-term effects. "There are actually two considerations," the strategist said. "The second is the volatility of the spot rate itself." Last week and on Thursday, the MAS bought U.S. dollars to weaken the Singapore dollar, but the rise in the spot rate retraced within hours. On Friday, traders said the MAS bid the U.S. dollar up to around to S$1.5470 from about S$1.5370, but the greenback eased back to S$1.5430 by 0600 GMT. In the debt market, government bond prices rose following a weaker-than-expected November manufacturing report in the U.S. that sent Treasury prices higher. The Singapore government bond yield curve steepened slightly, and some investment banks are predicting the two- to 10-year spread to rise by more than 30 basis points by the end of 2007. "If you look to the U.S., we may be entering a period of monetary easing, and the yield curve should get steeper," said Lee Wee-Kok, a strategist at Standard Chartered. "In Singapore it will be comparable." -By John Jannarone, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4152; john.jannarone@dowjones.com -Edited by Mary de Wet -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020061201e2c1003pd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
